      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "Abstract In this 1956 essay by Georg Luk\u00e1cs, he describes the crucial role played by \u201cperspective\u201d in novelistic representation. Luk\u00e1cs distinguishes literary realism in its classical, critical, and (non-schematic) socialist forms by ascribing to them a sense of perspective, defined as the expression of an objective social tendency through the thoughts and actions of characters in the course of their manifold relations to one another. In representing the various perspectives of representative individual characters, form is given to both the characters themselves and to the social-historical totality within which they are situated. Furthermore, such giving of form is so far-reaching that it extends to expressing the developmental tendencies in the present social reality leading beyond that reality. Luk\u00e1cs provides two examples to illustrate this phenomenon. First, as depicted in Tolstoy\u2019s War and Peace, the relatively inchoate yet nevertheless progressive thoughts of certain members of the Russian nobility during the Napoleonic Wars are viewed by Luk\u00e1cs as a perspective that is anticipatory of the later Decembrist revolt. At the same time, Luk\u00e1cs emphasizes the \u201cmodesty\u201d of perspective, the necessity of portraying only one step of an objective development. Thus, Luk\u00e1cs praises Sholokhov\u2019s And Quiet Flows the Don precisely because it does not depict a Cossack village simply embracing socialism; instead, Sholokhov achieves the goal of a perspectivally realistic representation through the conclusion of the village merely reconciling itself to Soviet power. These examples serve to illustrate just how essential perspective is for Luk\u00e1cs, for it is the mechanism whereby form is given to both the individual characters portrayed in a realist novel and the historical processes implicit in the social reality within which those characters are portrayed.",
